    // index
    "ix.0": "Grade 5 · US History · §113.16 · Problem-Based Learning",
    "ix.1": "A New Life — the Immigration Question, 1914",
    "ix.2": "A Problem-Based Learning unit. Students step into New York Harbor in 1914, take on a stakeholder’s point of view, and work a real, ill-structured question — building from surface to deep to transfer learning. The teacher is a guide, not the answer key.",
    "ix.3": "<b>Driving question:</b> The year is 1914. Should the Baldoni family be allowed to build a new life here — and how? What would <i>you</i> do in their place, and in the place of the people who must decide?",
    "ix.4": "The three-phase path (do them in order)",
    "ix.5": "Problem-solving is a <b>transfer</b> move — it only works once students have knowledge to reason with. So the problem in Phase 3 is deliberately <b>gated</b> behind Phases 1 and 2.",
    "ix.6": "Build the knowledge",
    "ix.7": "Vocabulary, key facts about 1900s immigration, a jigsaw read, and map work. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.8": "Connect & organize",
    "ix.9": "Push–pull concept map, primary sources, points of view, and a structured argument. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.10": "Solve the problem",
